gn military  presence which today remains  conditions-based and while we've made  clear to the Taliban that we're prepared  to remove our forces I want to be clear  we've not yet agreed on a timeline to do  so there should come as no surprise but  sometimes our adversaries announced  things that just aren't true I want to  reassure you that we hold detailed  discussions with the government  Afghanistan in parallel with our talks  with the Taliban ambassador bass general  Miller ambassador Leila's I have worked  diligently to ensure that we are fully  aligned in our approach and it was great  to be here today with President Ghani  all sides agree that finalizing a u.s.  Taliban understanding on terrorism and  foreign true presence will open the door  to enter Afghan dialogue and negotiation  that next step is at the heart of our  effort we are not and will not negotiate  with the Taliban on behalf of the  government or people of Afghanistan  rather we're working to reach Afghans  together at the negotiating table decide  the future of their own country  collectively and when that table is put  together it'll be a large table it's  crucial to include not just the table  under the government also  representatives from opposition parties  civil society including women in youth  as for our next diplomatic steps we  welcome Germany's commitment with Qatar  to convene and enter Afghan dialogue  next month this is an opportunity for  Afghans to endorse the urgent need for  inter Afghan negotiations simultaneously  the United States is laying the  groundwork for inter Afghan negotiations  to begin as soon as possible ejective of  those negotiations is where Afghans to  agree on a timeline and a political  roadmap for reaching a comprehensive  peace agreement not America's world to  dictate the outcome of those  positions we've been clear that's the  success or failure of the bilateral  relationship and Afghanistan's  relationship with the international  community including the donor community  we'll rest a part on what Afghans do to  maintain the civil rights of women and  minorities and preserve the gains of the  last 18 years I should add that not just  sharing the views of the United States  my words today reflect an international  consensus for peace my thanks go out to  neighboring countries who have supported  the process and which tend to benefit  tremendously from regional integration  that will for surely follow up from  peace we also sufficient appreciate the  support of our NATO allies and our  partners who serve alongside us and  resolute support his ongoing commitment  to the people of Afghanistan will be  critical in the post peace era the  trilateral consensus that we issued a  partnership with China and Russia as  well as the principles issued by the  United States Europe group or further  signs of the international community's  support for this peace process Pakistan  has a particularly important role to  play in this process and in the  promotion of peace and stability in the  region progress has been made and we  will continue to look to Pakistan for  practical measures cooperation on peace  talks and the implementation of any  agreement and I've said the word peace  more than half a dozen times it's our  priority but the procedure piece should  not wait till the Afghan presidential  election election planning must go  forward without delay as we pursue the  peace that Afghans deserve I urged the  Afghan government the Independent  Election Commission and all political  stakeholders to take all necessary steps  to ensure that the elections are  credible as we work to facilitate a  political settlement to the war in  Afghanistan we're also working with the  Afghan government and the international  community to lay the groundwork for a  stable and prosperous post-settlement  future for Afghanistan that's why today  beyond the meetings that I had at the  palace I had a number of internal  meetings today to dig into plans for our  future robust diplomatic security and  development presence the United States  will help Afghans preserve the gains of  the past 18 years  by supporting States ability to govern  [Music]  we have a peace deal before September  1st that's certainly our mission  second last question I want to get to  which was look we understand that the  tariff will remain and President Trump  has made clear that ensuring the  terrorism wherever we find it something  [Music]  Hey  Thank You mr. secretary you've talked  about this in the past but can you give  us anything concrete about ways in which  Iran has increased their involvement in  Afghanistan to make the peace process  more difficult then secondly I'd like to  give you an opportunity to respond to  what Iran said today they said that the  president rouhani said that the White  House is quote afflicted by mental  retardation and quote and said that the  path toward diplomacy is closed with is  it sanctions surely as for your first  question I can't share a lot more with  you about  involvement here in Afghanistan I would  say this it is not in Iran best interest  to undermine this peace process I would  hope that they could see that that they  could see that every regional players  best interest that this peace process  move forward after 18 years this is this  is something that that makes no sense  for all of the countries in the region  in in western Afghanistan has a long  border with Iran and to have a  reconciliation process move forward here  would be in that country's best interest  and so we'll continue to do the security  measures that we need to do here in  Afghanistan should Iran seek to further  destabilize the on-the-ground activities  here but I hope that they will not also  choose not to undermine the political  process the reconciliation process in  which we're engaged I've been in  meetings all day I hadn't seen the  statements that you that you read to me  today from the Iranians I can only say  